PROVENANCE
==========
Cited work: D. Perekrestenko, L. Eberhard and H. Bölcskei, "High-Dimensional Distribution Generation Through
  Deep Neural Networks", arXiv:2107.12466v3 (Aug. 2022; the version the citing paper dates "Aug. 2022").
Copy read: the arXiv e-print (TeX source) of 2107.12466v3, fetched 2026-10-14 from https://arxiv.org/e-print/2107.12466v3.
Locator: Section VIII ("Approximation of arbitrary distributions on bounded subsets of R^d with generative ReLU
  networks"), its first theorem; the class numbers theorems within sections (\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}[section],
  IEEEtran), so it is Theorem VIII.1 (\label{thm:main result on [0,1]}). Copied from the TeX source, verbatim.

\begin{theorem} \label{thm:main result on [0,1]}
For every distribution $\nu$ supported on $[0,1]^d$, there exists a $\frac{\delta}{n}$-quantized ReLU network $\Phi \in \cNN_{1,d}$ with $\mathcal{M}(\Phi) = \mathcal{O}(n^{d} + sn^{d-1})$ and $\mathcal{L}(\Phi)=(s+3)d - s -1$ such that 
\begin{equation}
	W(\Phi \# U, \nu) \leq \frac{\sqrt{d}}{n2^s} + \frac{2\sqrt{d}}{n} + \frac{d(d+1)}{2} (n-1) \delta. \label{main-result-w}
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

Following text of the same section (the specialization the citing paper uses, "let s = 1/ceil(n(n-1))"):
We can simplify the bound (\ref{main-result-w}) by setting $\delta = \frac{1}{\lceil \sqrt{d} (d+1) n (n-1) \rceil}$ (which satisfies the requirement $\delta < \frac{1}{n(n-1)}$ guaranteeing that all quantized weights are positive) to obtain, for $n \geq 2$,
\begin{equation}
	W(\Phi \# U, \nu) \leq \frac{\sqrt{d}}{n2^s} + \frac{2 \sqrt{d}}{n} + \frac{\sqrt{d}}{2n} = \frac{\sqrt{d}}{n2^s} + \frac{5 \sqrt{d}}{2n}.
\end{equation}
